Using Vieta's formulas, the sum of the roots $a+b+c=6$. The product of the roots $abc=-1$. We can use these to find the value of $a^2 b + b^2 c + c^2 a$ by considering the polynomial $x^3 - 6x^2 + 3x + 1$. By Vieta's formulas, we have:
\begin{align*}
a + b + c &= 6, \\
ab + ac + bc &= 3, \\
abc &= 1.
\end{align*}
The expression $a^2b + b^2c + c^2a$ can be rewritten as $(a^3 + b^3 + c^3) - (ab^2 + bc^2 + ca^2)$. Using the identity $a^3 + b^3 + c^3 = (a + b + c)(a^2 + b^2 + c^2 - ab - ac - bc) + 3abc$, and knowing that $a^2 + b^2 + c^2 = (a + b + c)^2 - 2(ab + ac + bc)$, we can calculate the desired value.
\begin{align*}
a^2 + b^2 + c^2 &= (a + b + c)^2 - 2(ab + ac + bc) \\
&= 6^2 - 2(3) \\
&= 36 - 6 \\
&= 30.
\end{align*}
\begin{align*}
a^3 + b^3 + c^3 &= (a + b + c)(a^2 + b^2 + c^2 - ab - ac - bc) + 3abc \\
&= 6(30 - 3) + 3 \\
&= 168 + 3 \\
&= 171.
\end{align*}
\begin{align*}
ab^2 + bc^2 + ca^2 &= (ab + ac + bc)(a + b + c) - (a^2b + b^2c + c^2a) \\
&= 3(6) - (a^2b + b^2c + c^2a) \\
&= 18 - (a^2b + b^2c + c^2a).
\end{align*}
Substituting these values into the identity for $a^2b + b^2c + c^2a$, we get:
\begin{align*}
a^2b + b^2c + c^2a &= (a^3 + b^3 + c^3) - (ab^2 + bc^2 + ca^2) \\
&= 171 - (18 - (a^2b + b^2c + c^2a)) \\
&= 153 - (a^2b + b^2c + c^2a).
\end{align*}
Therefore, $a^2b + b^2c + c^2a = 153$.
